# auditd rule for the Cerberus VSOCK-connect detective control
# (docs/vsock-connect-detection.md, Option A). Install to
# /etc/audit/rules.d/61-cerberus-vsock.rules (the numeric prefix follows
# auditd's convention of loading rules.d files in lexical order; 61 places it
# after the distro-default rule files, which is not load-bearing here but
# keeps it out of the way of any numbering convention already in use).
#
# This rule logs EVERY connect(2), including from the packaged ssh-cert-api
# binary itself: it does not filter by exe. auditd cannot filter on socket
# address family in the rule itself, so cerberus-vsock-watch's auditd path
# (vsockwatch/audit.go) does the real filtering — decoding each correlated
# SYSCALL+SOCKADDR pair, keeping only a genuine AF_VSOCK connect to the
# enclave's (CID, port), and classifying THAT against the full exe+uid+cgroup
# allowlist (Allowlist.Classify). Excluding the expected exe path at the rule
# level (an earlier version of this rule used `-F exe!=/usr/bin/ssh-cert-api`)
# would silently hide exactly the attack this control exists to catch: an
# attacker who runs (or bind-mounts) that same binary path under the wrong uid
# or outside cerberus-api.service's cgroup — Allowlist.Classify would call
# that Anomalous, but AuditWatcher would never even see the connect(2) to
# classify, since the rule itself would have discarded it first. A misconfigured
# or absent rule degrades to "cerberus-vsock-watch has fewer audit log lines to
# parse", not a silent gap in the eBPF detector, which is independent of this
# rule.
#
# After editing rule files, reload with: augenrules --load (or
# `service auditd restart` on distros without augenrules). Verify with:
#   auditctl -l | grep cerberus_vsock_watch
# cerberus-vsock-watch's TamperWatch (vsockwatch/tamper.go) periodically runs
# this same check and alerts if a previously-observed rule disappears.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S connect -k cerberus_vsock_watch
-a always,exit -F arch=b32 -S connect -k cerberus_vsock_watch
